WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.650
Hello and welcome everyone. I'm James Murphy.

00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:06.160
This&nbsp;GUI is created with the Dear ImGui GUI library.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:09.883
It's a minimalistic, easy to use but highly&nbsp;functional library.

00:00:09.883 --> 00:00:13.781
It can do menus, it can add and remove new windows on the fly,

00:00:13.781 --> 00:00:16.168
thanks to my patrons&nbsp;and donors by the way,

00:00:16.168 --> 00:00:18.653
it can nest regions within each other,

00:00:18.653 --> 00:00:22.454
you can have buttons and tool tips,&nbsp;menus of these sub windows,

00:00:22.454 --> 00:00:25.760
pop-ups, modal pop-ups,

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:27.598
drag and drop,

00:00:27.598 --> 00:00:30.761
tabs, tables

00:00:30.761 --> 00:00:32.727
and so much more.

00:00:32.727 --> 00:00:36.772
Here's&nbsp;a demo image of some of the more creative things you can do.

00:00:36.772 --> 00:00:40.513
But ImGui is a C++ library and this&nbsp;is a Python video.

00:00:40.513 --> 00:00:44.985
If your project is already in C++, then great. It's really easy to&nbsp;use.

00:00:44.985 --> 00:00:47.573
But there are a lot of great features and libraries of Python

00:00:47.573 --> 00:00:51.519
that are really just&nbsp;wrappers around C code or C++ code.

00:00:51.519 --> 00:00:55.966
Take NumPy or Pandas or most of the&nbsp;built-in functions in Python for example.

00:00:55.966 --> 00:00:58.516
Well, we're in luck. Enter pyimgui.

00:00:58.516 --> 00:01:02.147
pyimgui is&nbsp;an easy to install Python wrapper around ImGui.

00:01:02.147 --> 00:01:05.702
There's some basic boilerplate code you can copy&nbsp;paste to get things started.

00:01:05.702 --> 00:01:09.496
You need to initialize the library, ask the operating system for a window,

00:01:09.496 --> 00:01:12.591
in this case, I'm using the glfw library.

00:01:12.591 --> 00:01:15.083
tell ImGui that you want to render to that window,

00:01:15.083 --> 00:01:17.365
pick your&nbsp;favorite font or just use the default

00:01:17.365 --> 00:01:21.680
and then like most graphical applications, the rest of&nbsp;the time is spent in some kind of render loop.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:24.336
Each iteration of the render loop is pretty simple.

00:01:24.336 --> 00:01:27.346
We process any mouse or keyboard inputs and start a new frame.

00:01:27.346 --> 00:01:28.569
Clear the screen.

00:01:28.569 --> 00:01:31.700
Then here's where&nbsp;you're going to put most of your ImGui commands that define the GUI.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.632
After all the commands&nbsp;then you render.

00:01:33.632 --> 00:01:36.787
These frame_commands are going to hold all the actual interesting GUI logic.

00:01:36.787 --> 00:01:41.401
That's all the boilerplate you need. If we didn't put anything else, we basically just get&nbsp;this blank window.

00:01:41.401 --> 00:01:44.192
Now, look how easy it is to add a new window to your GUI.

00:01:44.192 --> 00:01:46.221
You just say begin and&nbsp;give it a name.

00:01:46.221 --> 00:01:49.264
Put whatever it is you want inside the window and then call end.

00:01:49.264 --> 00:01:51.904
That's all it takes&nbsp;and now we have a window.

00:01:51.904 --> 00:01:57.342
Windows are draggable, resizable and collapsible by default.

00:01:57.342 --> 00:02:00.339
Of course,&nbsp;there are extra flags and things that you can do to change how it behaves.

00:02:00.339 --> 00:02:03.943
Let's go ahead and&nbsp;add a main menu bar that has, like, a file close.

00:02:03.943 --> 00:02:05.446
And this is all it takes.

00:02:05.446 --> 00:02:06.837
I say begin_main_menu_bar

00:02:06.837 --> 00:02:10.534
which is going to return to me a boolean of whether or not the menu is displayed.

00:02:10.534 --> 00:02:13.221
If the menu&nbsp;is displayed, then I make a file menu.

00:02:13.221 --> 00:02:15.262
In the file menu, I make a quit item.

00:02:15.262 --> 00:02:19.040
Every frame when this&nbsp;runs, this returns to me whether or not it was clicked or selected.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.214
And then if you click quit, I&nbsp;just exit.

00:02:21.214 --> 00:02:24.080
We run it and now we have our file menu.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:28.042
This Ctrl+Q is just shortcut text. It doesn't&nbsp;actually do anything though.

00:02:28.042 --> 00:02:32.178
If I want to actually make the Ctrl+Q shortcut work globally, then I&nbsp;can just do this.

00:02:32.178 --> 00:02:36.143
This get_io object exposes keyboard and mouse events and state to you.

00:02:36.143 --> 00:02:39.678
So, I just check&nbsp;if Ctrl and the Q key are down and then exit.

00:02:39.678 --> 00:02:42.999
As you can hopefully see by now, the appeal of&nbsp;ImGui is how little code

00:02:42.999 --> 00:02:46.357
you actually have to write in order to get something extremely&nbsp;functional.

00:02:46.357 --> 00:02:50.000
And one of its biggest selling points is, its declarative style.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.798
In a lot of GUI&nbsp;frameworks, you have to start with some kind of initialization method

00:02:53.798 --> 00:02:58.214
where you specify all of the&nbsp;possible different widgets and menus and things that you're going to have.

00:02:58.214 --> 00:03:00.225
Then you would hook up&nbsp;some kind of event handlers

00:03:00.225 --> 00:03:03.259
that will enable and disable components depending on what you do.

00:03:03.259 --> 00:03:05.862
But&nbsp;with ImGui, you don't have to do any of that.

00:03:05.862 --> 00:03:08.358
If I want a button that when you click it, it opens&nbsp;a pop-up,

00:03:08.358 --> 00:03:10.987
I just say: if button, open_popup.

00:03:10.987 --> 00:03:14.289
Then I check if the popup is open and put whatever I&nbsp;want inside.

00:03:14.289 --> 00:03:16.612
And there you have it. Click, popup.

00:03:16.612 --> 00:03:19.804
I never needed to create a button object or set up&nbsp;any event handlers.

00:03:19.804 --> 00:03:24.401
I just read the ImGui commands top to bottom and do anything that I need to do as&nbsp;it happens.

00:03:24.401 --> 00:03:28.285
You just declare what you want to see and let ImGui take care of the widget bookkeeping.

00:03:28.285 --> 00:03:32.590
As it stands, ImGui and pyimgui are already great libraries.

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:36.560
I'd recommend them to anyone who wants&nbsp;a quick and easy way to get a GUI up and running.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:43.120
But at its heart, ImGui is a C++ library and some&nbsp;of the C++isms are leaking through here.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:46.567
Every time I begin a window, I need to make sure&nbsp;to end it.

00:03:46.567 --> 00:03:51.659
Begin a popup, then you need to remember to end it. But only if the if returned true.

00:03:51.659 --> 00:03:55.403
And&nbsp;there are a whole bunch of these components that I declare with some begin something

00:03:55.403 --> 00:03:57.905
and then I&nbsp;need to remember to end them.

00:03:57.905 --> 00:04:03.917
I have a resource that I open and then need to remember to manually&nbsp;close.

00:04:03.917 --> 00:04:06.592
Does that sound like a familiar pattern to you?

00:04:06.592 --> 00:04:12.000
It's kind of like writing to a file. I need&nbsp;to open the file, write to it and then call close.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.607
Except I guess, if you forget to close a file, that&nbsp;might not be that much of an issue.

00:04:15.607 --> 00:04:18.395
You might not even notice it depending on the program.

00:04:18.395 --> 00:04:21.099
But let's&nbsp;say, I forget to end this popup.

00:04:21.099 --> 00:04:24.551
Well, everything is fine until the popup is supposed to appear.

00:04:24.551 --> 00:04:26.353
Then&nbsp;I get a crash.

00:04:26.353 --> 00:04:29.429
So, unlike with closing files, if you want the render loop to continue,

00:04:29.429 --> 00:04:33.840
You absolutely&nbsp;must close every begin with its corresponding end.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.508
Okay. So, just get better at programming. Don't&nbsp;forget your ends.

00:04:37.508 --> 00:04:41.447
Well, that is a generally convincing argument but that's still not good&nbsp;enough.

00:04:41.447 --> 00:04:44.286
Besides the fact that it's annoying to have to remember

00:04:44.286 --> 00:04:47.583
sometimes it's not possible due&nbsp;to exceptions.

00:04:47.583 --> 00:04:51.366
What if I was supposed to read this popup text from a file but the file got deleted.

00:04:51.366 --> 00:04:55.100
I can even try to catch the exception. But at that point, it's too late.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:58.936
Even with the except Exception,&nbsp;when I click the button, I still get a crash.

00:04:58.936 --> 00:05:01.586
And notice, it did catch that exception.

00:05:01.586 --> 00:05:04.753
The exception that crashed the program was a different one.

00:05:04.753 --> 00:05:07.636
The exception that crashed the program was from&nbsp;when the render method

00:05:07.636 --> 00:05:11.685
at the end of the render loop goes in and tries to consolidate everything.

00:05:11.685 --> 00:05:15.694
And it notices that there was one extra begin that had no end.

00:05:15.694 --> 00:05:18.938
And it doesn't know how to fix that. So,&nbsp;it has to just give up and crash.

00:05:18.938 --> 00:05:21.718
This would be the same story as if you were using a file.

00:05:21.718 --> 00:05:24.689
You open&nbsp;it, you write to it and then some exception occurs.

00:05:24.689 --> 00:05:26.371
Well, then the file doesn't get closed.

00:05:26.371 --> 00:05:30.257
But Python&nbsp;has a solution for this. It's context managers, the with statement.

00:05:30.257 --> 00:05:32.303
I say with open as file.

00:05:32.303 --> 00:05:36.900
Then I&nbsp;can do my using. And then even if an exception is raised, the file will still be closed.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:40.000
Well, that's&nbsp;exactly what I want for the pyimgui library.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.956
I want to say with window, forget about the end.

00:05:42.956 --> 00:05:46.695
I&nbsp;want that to happen automatically and then just indent everything.

00:05:46.695 --> 00:05:49.285
I want to say with begin popup&nbsp;as popup,

00:05:49.285 --> 00:05:51.395
if the popup's opened do whatever,

00:05:51.395 --> 00:05:56.127
and even if there's an exception, I want to&nbsp;make sure that in all cases the proper end function is called.

00:05:56.127 --> 00:05:59.407
Okay. Well, ImGui is&nbsp;an open source library. Let's do it.

00:05:59.407 --> 00:06:03.440
Even though it's an open source project, I'm not&nbsp;trying to steamroll over someone's garden here.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:06.052
So first, I made an issue on their Github.

00:06:06.052 --> 00:06:10.405
I explain&nbsp;my reasoning and use case. I explain what I'd like to be able to write.

00:06:10.405 --> 00:06:14.345
And I go over a potential&nbsp;implementation of how this could be achieved.

00:06:14.345 --> 00:06:17.185
And look at that, we get the go ahead from the&nbsp;project maintainer.

00:06:17.185 --> 00:06:19.939
Step two: Create my own fork of the library and clone it.

00:06:19.939 --> 00:06:22.506
I create a new branch&nbsp;for the feature that I'm adding.

00:06:22.506 --> 00:06:26.728
Make sure that you're branching off of the development branch, not&nbsp;the master branch.

00:06:26.728 --> 00:06:30.569
With ImGui, these were hundreds of commits apart. So, make sure you're using the right&nbsp;one.

00:06:30.569 --> 00:06:32.502
This is ImGui's main file.

00:06:32.502 --> 00:06:36.706
It is incredibly long, thousands and thousands and thousands of lines.

00:06:36.706 --> 00:06:39.423
It&nbsp;looks like we have eleven thousand something lines here.

00:06:39.423 --> 00:06:44.699
And it's not just eleven thousand lines of Python, it's eleven thousand lines of Cython.

00:06:44.699 --> 00:06:49.740
Cython is an implementation of Python that compiles into&nbsp;C or C++ code.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:55.575
It also allows you to import and call raw C and C++ functions from&nbsp;within Python.

00:06:55.575 --> 00:06:59.428
This is what allows us to use ImGui at all. Remember, it's a C++ library.

00:06:59.428 --> 00:07:01.959
Cython allows you to write normal Python code.

00:07:01.959 --> 00:07:06.118
But it also allows you to write this weird mixture of C&nbsp;like Python code.

00:07:06.118 --> 00:07:10.422
Here, we define a class DrawList. But we use this cdef keyword.

00:07:10.422 --> 00:07:15.173
This is going to tell&nbsp;Cython to create a C extension class instead of a normal Python class.

00:07:15.173 --> 00:07:19.005
This is not meant to&nbsp;be a Cython tutorial. Cython is a huge topic.

00:07:19.005 --> 00:07:22.960
I just want to wet your appetite a little bit&nbsp;and show you some of the things that can be done.

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:27.920
Anything that uses the cdef keyword is going&nbsp;to use C rather than Python under the hood.

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:32.480
That allows you to manipulate things like pointers&nbsp;that you couldn't manipulate in raw Python.

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:34.910
You can also mark functions with cdef.

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:38.609
Functions&nbsp;marked this way can only be called from within other Cython code.

00:07:38.609 --> 00:07:41.760
That means this method&nbsp;would not be accessible to regular Python.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:45.867
This can actually be a really good thing because&nbsp;cdef functions and variables and so on

00:07:45.867 --> 00:07:51.028
can call each other and use each other without ever&nbsp;returning control back to the Python interpreter.

00:07:51.028 --> 00:07:55.464
If I have two cdef integers, I can add&nbsp;them and that addition will happen in C.

00:07:55.464 --> 00:08:01.525
Cython also makes it easy to convert back and&nbsp;forth between Python objects and C counterparts.

00:08:01.525 --> 00:08:05.032
Notice, here's a function that says it takes a&nbsp;float width.

00:08:05.032 --> 00:08:08.327
Now, this is not using the normal variable annotation syntax.

00:08:08.327 --> 00:08:10.620
This is special&nbsp;Cython syntax.

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:12.476
This is a normal Python function.

00:08:12.476 --> 00:08:17.701
And Cython will actually try to convert whatever&nbsp;you pass as this argument into a float.

00:08:17.701 --> 00:08:23.520
So, if you pass in a Python float, it will unwrap that value&nbsp;and give you the C floating point value out of it.

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:28.808
And if you try to return a C float back&nbsp;to Python land, it will get wrapped up in a Python object.

00:08:28.808 --> 00:08:32.960
Let's get back on task. Here's&nbsp;the begin function that starts a window.

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:35.327
And here's the end function.

00:08:35.327 --> 00:08:40.377
Besides, like a 100 lines&nbsp;of documentation, these are actually really really short functions.

00:08:40.377 --> 00:08:44.640
I basically just call the raw C&nbsp;version of the function and then return its value.

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:48.445
There's a little bit of finessing since the&nbsp;raw C version actually uses out parameters

00:08:48.445 --> 00:08:51.778
in order to fill this value of whether or not&nbsp;it was opened.

00:08:51.778 --> 00:08:55.423
The C library returns to whether or not the window was expanded

00:08:55.423 --> 00:08:59.235
and then it uses&nbsp;an out parameter to tell you whether or not the window was opened.

00:08:59.235 --> 00:09:03.347
And end is even simpler.&nbsp;It just calls the raw C ImGui end function

00:09:03.347 --> 00:09:07.304
Here's the idea. Right now, this is just returning a&nbsp;tuple of two booleans.

00:09:07.304 --> 00:09:12.410
My idea is to instead return a tuple like object that has the same information

00:09:12.410 --> 00:09:16.335
but also has enter and exit methods for with support.

00:09:16.335 --> 00:09:18.082
So, let's construct this class.

00:09:18.082 --> 00:09:21.479
First off,&nbsp;we want it to be fast. We're going to use a cdef class.

00:09:21.479 --> 00:09:23.661
I preface the class name with an&nbsp;underscore to indicate that

00:09:23.661 --> 00:09:26.720
you should not be trying to create one of these from Python.

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:29.992
This is a private implementation detail of ImGui.

00:09:29.992 --> 00:09:35.977
I have this explicitly inherit from object.&nbsp;This is just because ImGui is trying to support Python 2.7.

00:09:35.977 --> 00:09:39.000
My original proposal&nbsp;used things like typing named tuples.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:42.895
But because they want to support all the way back to Python 2.7, I'm going to do it the old school way

00:09:42.895 --> 00:09:45.345
I make expanded and opened.

00:09:45.345 --> 00:09:48.608
These are going to be&nbsp;the two tuple values begin was already returning.

00:09:48.608 --> 00:09:52.458
I mark them as bools. So, they'll automatically&nbsp;be converted to Python booleans.

00:09:52.458 --> 00:09:54.763
And I mark them as readonly.

00:09:54.763 --> 00:09:58.211
What this does is, it allows&nbsp;Python code to access these attributes.

00:09:58.211 --> 00:10:01.063
But it only allows them to read them not write to them.

00:10:01.063 --> 00:10:04.549
I&nbsp;could use a regular init but I'm going to use this cinit.

00:10:04.549 --> 00:10:08.769
This is just for a trick I know to make it&nbsp;fast. You could just as well just use regular init.

00:10:08.769 --> 00:10:12.247
Then the heart and soul of the proposal enter and&nbsp;exit methods.

00:10:12.247 --> 00:10:15.185
This is what allows you to use the with statement.

00:10:15.185 --> 00:10:17.923
It's pretty typical for enter to&nbsp;just return self.

00:10:17.923 --> 00:10:21.812
Then in the exit method, we always just call the cimgui's end function.

00:10:21.812 --> 00:10:25.040
regardless of&nbsp;whether or not there was an exception or whatever.

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:29.740
If there was an exception, it will call this&nbsp;function and then just continue propagating the exception.

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:32.176
Next, I'm going to add a get item and&nbsp;iter.

00:10:32.176 --> 00:10:34.901
Personally, I don't think this class should have these methods.

00:10:34.901 --> 00:10:36.457
But it's too late.

00:10:36.457 --> 00:10:39.892
pyimgui is&nbsp;an existing library that already has lots of users.

00:10:39.892 --> 00:10:42.701
Prior to this point, the return value of begin was&nbsp;a tuple.

00:10:42.701 --> 00:10:46.535
So, it needs to maintain that behavior in order to not break old code.

00:10:46.535 --> 00:10:49.351
So, we definitely need&nbsp;to be able to index into the tuple

00:10:49.351 --> 00:10:52.455
and the iter method is for allowing variable unpacking.

00:10:52.455 --> 00:10:56.556
Then&nbsp;since I'm such an upstanding citizen, I write a repr for the class.

00:10:56.556 --> 00:10:59.240
And that's basically all&nbsp;the functionality that we need.

00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:03.558
So, we declared our attributes in a little funny way. We used cinit&nbsp;instead of init.

00:11:03.558 --> 00:11:06.298
But then everything else is kind of just a regular class.

00:11:06.298 --> 00:11:10.122
So, now let's use our begin&nbsp;end as the return value instead of just a tuple.

00:11:11.907 --> 00:11:16.477
I just take whatever the existing return value&nbsp;was and wrap it in my BeginEnd class.

00:11:16.477 --> 00:11:18.525
But remember that speed hack I mentioned.

00:11:18.525 --> 00:11:23.016
Well, instead of&nbsp;instantiating the class the normal way, we're going to use the dunder new method.

00:11:23.016 --> 00:11:25.793
For sneaky reasons,&nbsp;this is going to be faster.

00:11:25.793 --> 00:11:28.745
Also, we didn't define a regular init. So, we can't call it.

00:11:28.745 --> 00:11:32.065
And that's all we&nbsp;have to do for the begin function.

00:11:32.065 --> 00:11:37.364
Now I just grep for all the def begins that there are in the file&nbsp;and this is the work that I have cut out for me.

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:44.742
What I showed you was what I had to do for&nbsp;begin. Now I just need to repeat it for the other 17 others.

00:11:44.742 --> 00:11:45.647
And there are so many of these.

00:11:45.647 --> 00:11:47.755
I know there's no way I can do it without making a typo

00:11:47.755 --> 00:11:50.841
so I actually made test cases for all of them.

00:11:50.841 --> 00:11:53.931
My process was to write test cases for one of the pairs

00:11:53.931 --> 00:11:55.684
check that the tests fail

00:11:55.684 --> 00:12:01.695
then I would&nbsp;implement the new functionality, recompile and run the tests to see that they pass or didn't.

00:12:01.695 --> 00:12:04.663
Finally,&nbsp;I would add an updated working version of my demo

00:12:04.663 --> 00:12:07.614
using the with statement to see that&nbsp;everything is working.

00:12:07.614 --> 00:12:12.139
Then so on and so forth for the next set of tests and the&nbsp;next set of tests and the next set of tests

00:12:12.139 --> 00:12:18.099
And so on. So, this is actually, I'm recording this at the very end and these are all the test cases.

00:12:18.099 --> 00:12:22.377
Just so you can see what it would look like,&nbsp;first I set cythonize with coverage equal to one

00:12:22.377 --> 00:12:26.610
With this on, it'll compile in debug mode&nbsp;and the compilations will go way faster.

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:29.847
Then I pip install the current directory which&nbsp;will trigger the Cython build.

00:12:29.847 --> 00:12:33.502
It still took 30 seconds or so to compile but that's just how&nbsp;it goes.

00:12:33.502 --> 00:12:39.049
Then I just run py test on my test file. And all 61 tests pass.

00:12:39.049 --> 00:12:40.641
And let's head back to our&nbsp;example from before.

00:12:40.641 --> 00:12:44.616
Remember I had this window where I click a button and I get a popup and it&nbsp;crashes.

00:12:44.616 --> 00:12:46.521
Well, now we're using the with statement.

00:12:46.521 --> 00:12:49.539
So, even though we're raising the exception which we&nbsp;do catch here,

00:12:49.539 --> 00:12:53.745
we should find that the end popup will be correctly called and we won't get a crash.

00:12:53.745 --> 00:12:57.059
So, we click the button and we see caught exception no crash.

00:12:57.059 --> 00:13:00.634
And then once the popup goes away, then&nbsp;that stuff stops printing out.

00:13:00.634 --> 00:13:03.091
All right, great. Just one more thing before submitting the pull&nbsp;requests.

00:13:03.091 --> 00:13:04.534
We just need to update the doc-

00:13:04.534 --> 00:13:06.935
...

